<edge source="global_currency_market_s::fx_exchange_rate_level" target="iran_hormuz_flashpoint::oil_price_volatility" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Appreciation of a major invoicing currency (e.g., USD) reduces non‑dollar buyers' purchasing power and prompts rapid inventory and contract repricing, producing larger short‑term swings in crude prices tied to Gulf supply risks.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_currency_market_s</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_hormuz_flashpoint</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_currency_market_s::fx_exchange_rate_level" target="russia_ukraine_war::drone_strike_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A weaker domestic currency (e.g., a depreciating ruble) raises the ruble cost of imported components, spare parts and foreign-denominated logistics for long‑range and precision drones, slowing production/maintenance and lowering the tempo of drone strikes over months.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_currency_market_s</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_currency_market_s::fx_exchange_rate_level" target="us_china_tech_trade_riva::ai_infra_financing_vol" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">An increase in the key exchange rate (e.g., USD appreciation) raises the local‑currency cost of importing servers, GPUs and other USD‑priced equipment for import‑dependent jurisdictions, worsening project economics and causing financiers to scale back AI infrastructure commitments.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_currency_market_s</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_trade_riva</data>
</edge>
